From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 00:23:37 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Add RT-11 native REL object output for pdp11-uknc-rt11 (-m
 pdp11rt11rel)

New emulation, selected explicitly via `-m pdp11rt11rel`, meant to be
used with `-r`: writes RT-11's own "Relocatable Object Language" object
format (GSD/TXT/RLD/ENDMOD blocks, RADIX-50 names, documented in the
RT-11 Volume and File Formats Manual) instead of an a.out-pdp11 object.

Same two-stage design as pdp11rt11sav: OUTPUT_FORMAT stays
"a.out-pdp11" so the actual `-r` link goes through a.out-pdp11's own
proven relocation-resolution code, producing exactly the file
`ld -r -m pdp11rt11` would; once that file is fully written,
ld/emultempl/pdp11rt11rel.em's after_close_output hook reopens it and
reads its symbols/relocations/section contents back with ordinary
a.out-pdp11 BFD calls (bfd_canonicalize_symtab/reloc,
bfd_get_section_contents), then repacks all of that into RT-11's
native object format directly, without needing a full BFD target of
its own for either direction.

Implements: a RADIX-50 codec (6-char uppercase names, non-representable
characters such as C's leading '_' folded to '.'); GSD emission (module
name, one p-sect entry per .text/.data/.bss immediately followed by the
global symbols defined in it, then .ABS. followed by undefined global
references); TXT blocks chunked to 128 bytes (an RLD entry's
displacement byte only covers 0-255, relative to the immediately
preceding TXT block's own load address); and RLD entries covering all
three relocation kinds RT-11 distinguishes -- Internal (same p-sect),
P-sect (a different p-sect), and Global (external symbol) -- in both
their plain and additive/displaced forms, decided from the raw content
word already sitting at each relocation's address (a.out-pdp11 has no
separate stored addend) plus which of the three known sections the
target symbol's own section-relative dummy symbol resolves to.

Verified against the RADIX-50/GSD/RLD field layouts in the RT-11
manual with an independent Python decoder (checksums, symbol names,
section sizes, and relocation classification all confirmed correct)
on a real two-object `-r` link exercising a still-undefined external
symbol (___main), a same-section reference, and cross-section
references in both directions.

Includes what was a separate fix on top of this one, now part of it:

Fix pdp11rt11rel truncating the file it was still reading

pdp11rel_write opened the linked output "wb" to write the REL module
over it, while ibfd was still open on that same path: rel_write_section
reads each section's contents and relocations out of it as it goes.
Every read past the point BFD had already cached failed with "file
truncated", so whether a link worked depended on file size and section
order -- it surfaced as an intermittent failure on a PPU program that
had merely grown a little.

Write to a temporary file and rename it over the original once the
reader is done with it.
